{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2377 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c67493761c58e325ecb945c0b4170fdcaf0ef6cb20cc2273b8fd0d947a125af1" :edb/db-before-t 2376 :edb/db-after-t 2377 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535689 50 #inst "2026-10-09T05:13:24.908Z" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124191 1100 "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j$, compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))]$." 13194139535689 false] [17592186124191 1100 "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124191 1145 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(x^{2} + 2y^{3})i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(x^{2} + 2y^{3})j \\\\\n&= 2xi + 6y^{2}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(r(t)) &= 2(\\sin t)i + 6(e^{t})^{2}j \\\\\n&= 2\\sin ti + 6e^{2t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{\\prime }(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t})j \\\\\n&= \\cos ti + e^{t}j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= \\nabla f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{\\prime }(t) \\\\\n&= (2\\sin ti + 6e^{2t}j) \\cdot (\\cos ti + e^{t}j) \\\\\n&= 2\\sin t\\cos t + 6e^{2t} \\cdot e^{t} \\\\\n&= \\sin 2t + 6e^{3t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535689 false] [17592186124191 1145 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124192 1100 "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124192 1100 "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t \\, \\mathbf j +t^3 \\, \\mathbf k$, compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))]$." 13194139535689 false] [17592186124192 1145 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(x, y, z) &= \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial x}(xz\\ln y)i + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial y}(xz\\ln y)j + \\frac{\\partial }{\\partial z}(xz\\ln y)k \\\\\n&= z\\ln yi + \\frac{xz}{y}j + x\\ln yk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2$, $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla f(r(t)) &= t^{3}\\ln ti + t^{4}j + t^{2}\\ln tk\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{\\prime }(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j + \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3})k \\\\\n&= 2ti + j + 3t^{2}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))] &= \\nabla f(r(t)) \\cdot r^{\\prime }(t) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}\\ln ti + t^{4}j + t^{2}\\ln tk) \\cdot (2ti + j + 3t^{2}k) \\\\\n&= (t^{3}\\ln t) \\cdot 2t + (t^{4}) \\cdot 1 + (t^{2}\\ln t) \\cdot 3t^{2} \\\\\n&= 2t^{4}\\ln t + t^{4} + 3t^{4}\\ln t \\\\\n&= t^{4}(5\\ln t + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535689 false] [17592186124192 1145 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{xz}{y}\\,j+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124193 1100 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124193 1100 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4$." 13194139535689 false] [17592186124193 1145 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124193 1145 "Use $\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)$. Here $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\langle2x,-2y,-1\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'=\\langle\\cos t,-\\sin t,1\\rangle$. Thus $$\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\langle2\\sin t,-2\\cos t,-1\\rangle\\cdot\\langle\\cos t,-\\sin t,1\\rangle=4\\sin t\\cos t-1.$$ Evaluating at $t=\\pi/4$ gives $4(\\sqrt2/2)^2-1=1$." 13194139535689 false] [17592186124322 1100 "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j$." 13194139535689 false] [17592186124322 1100 "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124322 1145 "The chain rule gives $\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))=\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)$. At $t=\\pi$, $\\mathbf r(\\pi)=(0,-1)$, so $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(\\pi))=\\langle-3,-1\\rangle$. Also $\\mathbf r'(\\pi)=\\langle\\cos\\pi,-\\sin\\pi\\rangle=\\langle-1,0\\rangle$. Therefore the required rate is $(-3)(-1)+(-1)(0)=3$." 13194139535689 false] [17592186124322 1145 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361418 13194139535689 true] [17592186124387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361460 13194139535689 true] [17592186124387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361467 13194139535689 true] [17592186124387 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361474 13194139535689 true] [17592186124388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361481 13194139535689 true] [17592186124388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361488 13194139535689 true] [17592186124388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361495 13194139535689 true] [17592186124388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361502 13194139535689 true] [17592186124388 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361509 13194139535689 true] [17592186124389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361404 13194139535689 true] [17592186124389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361411 13194139535689 true] [17592186124389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361439 13194139535689 true] [17592186124389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361446 13194139535689 true] [17592186124389 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361453 13194139535689 true] [17592186124514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361390 13194139535689 true] [17592186124514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361397 13194139535689 true] [17592186124514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361425 13194139535689 true] [17592186124514 1020 #edb/ref 17592186361432 13194139535689 true] [17592186124871 1100 "If $f(x, y) = x^{2} + y^{2}$ and $\\mathbf{r} ( t ) = {e}^{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} + {e}^{- t} \\, \\mathbf{j} ,$, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d} t} [ f ( \\mathbf{r} ( t ) ) ] .$." 13194139535689 false] [17592186124871 1100 "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" 13194139535689 true] [17592186124871 1145 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j\\end{ali